Republican column", "Prediction markets on Kalshi and Polymarket give Ahlers only about a 3% win chance", "No public general-election polling and no signs of a challenger financial edge"], "rationale": "South Dakota's strong Republican lean, Rhoden's incumbency, unanimous Solid/Safe Republican ratings from six raters, and markets pricing Ahlers near three percent all point the same direction. With no public polling showing a contest, the race stays safely Republican.", "watch": "Any late independent polling putting Ahlers within single digits, or a fundraising surge or major scandal involving Rhoden, would be the first sign of a competitive race.", "confidence": "high", "notes": ["No usable general-election polls; forecast rests on fundamentals and expert ratings.", "Incumbent Larry Rhoden (R) is running."]}, "overview": "South Dakota's governor's race pits incumbent Republican Larry Rhoden, seeking his first full term, against Democratic nominee Dan Ahlers, a former state legislator. The state leans Republican by roughly fifteen points, and no public general-election polls have been released. Six expert raters unanimously place the race in the Solid or Safe Republican column, and prediction markets on Kalshi and Polymarket give Ahlers about a three percent chance of winning.\n\nThe candidates have met in several debates, including at the South Dakota State Fair, where both criticized federal beef import policy but clashed elsewhere. Rhoden has campaigned alongside the Republican ticket and holds the advantages of incumbency in a deeply favorable environment. Ahlers has emphasized his legislative and agricultural background, but with no polling showing a competitive race and markets pricing a Republican win as near-certain, the race stands as safely Republican heading into the final weeks.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.0185, "margin": -14.12}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.0209, "margin": -15.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.0249, "margin"